#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913} :activity/id #uuid "7247ca88-471f-5303-aa77-7e868bf58346" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121135 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle t+1, \\: t^2 +1\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this function, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'(t) = \\langle 1, \\: 2t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this function and its derivative at $t=0,$ we get the following pair of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf f'(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nIf we view $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ as a vector attached to the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf f(0),$ then it will be tangent to the curve $C,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/854d16c788836f784d739f73063354fa3ca2ad233f03cce53b99e5764449f585.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf f'(0) \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ it serves as a direction vector for the tangent line to the curve at $\\mathbf f(0).$ The equation of this line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u) & =f(0)+u{f}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+u⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+u,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, if a curve $C$ is parametrized by a vector function $\\mathbf f(t),$ then the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ gives a **tangent vector** to $C$ at $\\mathbf{f} (t),$ provided that $\\mathbf f'(t)\\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFor a fixed $t$ in the domain of $\\mathbf{f}(t),$ the equation of the corresponding tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{R}(u) = \\mathbf{f}(t) + u\\mathbf{f}'(t), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ always points in the direction that the curve $C$ is oriented. In our example above, as $t$ increases and passes through the point $(1,1),$ the curve is transversed from left to right, and the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ is also from left to right." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd32d96-f4fc-5f66-861c-fc1b9746be10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d2f798c-9ffb-57a0-aea4-4212795c1dcc" :step/math-academy-id 45344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119513 :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345045}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"} :step/id #uuid "989791d3-66ad-598a-a711-f5c1c0da6f59" :step/math-academy-id 45345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119514 :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345